// nparse.n — the M5 chain, token-native end to end: a complete toy
//   compiler with FUNCTIONS, REAL IDENTIFIERS, and — since the selfhost
//   road's rung 3 — a real AST for its expressions, with a real CHECK
//   PASS and a real TRANSFORM over it. lex → parse → check → fold →
//   emit → run, in N's own syntax:
//       fn fact(n) { r := 1; if n > 1 { r := n * fact(n - 1); } r } fact(5)
//   The lexer fills a token buffer (sbrk'd i64 arrays, v0.16 index
//   stores); the parser consumes TOKENS, never characters; the VM runs
//   the emitted stack code. The grammar covers the whole toy language:
//     program := { fndef } { stmt } cmp
//     fndef   := 'fn' name '(' name { ',' name } ')' '{' { stmt } cmp '}'
//     stmt    := name ':=' cmp ';'
//              | 'if' cmp block [ 'else' ( block | if-stmt ) ]
//              | 'while' cmp block
//              | 'print' cmp ';'      (strings are factors now)
//   `else if` CHAINS are free structure, not new machinery: the else
//   slot always held a statement index, and a nested if IS a
//   statement — the parser just recurses into ast_stmt instead of
//   demanding braces, and no walker changed at all.
//
//   THE WHOLE PROGRAM IS A TREE NOW. The old parser emitted code while
//   it descended — postfix fell out of parse order, which works
//   exactly as long as nothing needs to happen between understanding
//   the program and generating its code. A checker is precisely that
//   kind of thing: it must look at the program BEFORE code for it
//   exists, which is why ncc parses to Expr/Stmt trees first and an
//   emit-on-the-fly toy never could. So the parse rules BUILD a
//   postorder AST — expressions since rung 3, statements too since the
//   rung after — as parallel i64 node arrays carried in one struct
//   (Ast — nk kind, nl lhs, nr rhs, nv value, and na, a flat table
//   with four slots per node: call arguments, or an if's else arm).
//     node kind 0 = number (nv = value)     1 = load (nv = var slot)
//               2 = binop (nl/nr = children, nv = ITS VM OPCODE)
//               3 = negate (nl = operand)
//               4 = call (nv = fn symbol, nl = arg count, args in na)
//               5 = bind (nv = slot, nl = rhs)
//               6 = if (nl = cond, nr = then chain, na[0] = else or -1)
//               7 = while (nl = cond, nr = body chain)
//               8 = seq (nl = statement, nr = next link or -1)
//               9 = print (nl = expr; the operand's TYPE picks the
//                   opcode)                10 = string literal (nv)
//               11 = len (nl = a str operand; the measuring builtin)
//               12 = logical not (nl = an int operand; bare '!')
//               13 = itos (nl = an interpolation hole's expression;
//                    int in, its decimal text out)
//   A separate walker, emit_node, then emits by postorder recursion —
//   children first, the node's own operation last. Postfix is no
//   longer a lucky consequence of parse order; it is a property of the
//   tree, and the walker produces byte-for-byte the code the emitting
//   parser used to, so every result and word count below is unchanged:
//   the architecture moved under the outputs. The caller-save call
//   machinery (save the callee's slots in order, evaluate ALL the
//   arguments before binding any — so `gcd(b, a % b)` still reads the
//   OLD values — bind in REVERSE because pops hand back the last
//   argument first, CALL, restore in reverse with SWAP + STORE) moved
//   wholesale from the parser into the walker: parse decides WHAT (a
//   call of f with these argument trees), emit decides HOW — ncc's own
//   parse → gen split, now inside the toy. And the first dividend of
//   holding a whole call as data: the parser checks the argument count
//   against the callee's parameter count ("wrong number of
//   arguments"), a diagnostic the fused version had no natural seat
//   for. Statements joined the tree next: ast_stmt/ast_block build
//   bind/if/while nodes with blocks as seq CHAINS (a cons list in the
//   same arrays: nl = statement, nr = next), and the jump patching —
//   the if/else diamond, the while back-JMP — relocated wholesale into
//   the statement walker, emit_stmt. Only the outermost skeleton still
//   emits as it parses: a function's entry recording, its parameter
//   table, and the hop-over JMP past the fndefs — headers, not
//   meaning. Each BODY (a function's, or main's) is now one finished
//   tree, and the passes run over it in order: check, fold, emit.
//   One honest consequence: parse errors now surface before check
//   errors program-wide (a body parses completely before its passes
//   run), where the fused version interleaved them in source order.
//
//   THE CHECK PASS is the payoff the AST exists for. Between build and
//   emit, check_node walks the finished tree — it reads the program
//   and emits nothing — and rejects any load of a name that is not in
//   the BOUND SET: the enclosing function's parameters plus the names
//   bound by `:=` statements already compiled. Every node records its
//   source line (nline, the sixth Ast array), so the refusal is
//   located: "line N: error: unknown variable". Scope is honest and
//   conservative, and it lives entirely in the CHECK walk now: a seq
//   chain threads the bound count forward, a bind adds its name only
//   AFTER its right-hand side checks, and an if arm or while body
//   checks with the count it was given and hands back the ORIGINAL —
//   names born in a branch die with it, because the checker cannot
//   know the branch will have run (the same conservative rule ncc's
//   v0.18 own-move branch merge enforces, here in miniature). A function
//   body sees ONLY its parameters and its own bindings: the flat
//   runtime table lets bodies read anything, but that is caller-save's
//   private business, and the checker now enforces the lexical story.
//
//   THE FOLD PASS is the other thing a tree buys. fold_node walks the
//   same postorder and REWRITES: a binop whose children both folded to
//   numbers becomes a number computed at COMPILE time; a negation of a
//   number likewise; call arguments fold, calls never do. The fused
//   parser-emitter could not have done this even in principle — by the
//   time it had seen both operands, their PUSHes were already emitted;
//   rewriting history is exactly what holding the program AS DATA
//   makes possible. The folding demo compiles to 10 code words where
//   the unfolded tree emits 19. Division and modulo by a constant
//   zero are left unfolded: the compiler must not crash computing
//   what the program would — the VM owns that failure, at run time.
//   Since the statements joined the tree, the fold pass rewrites THEM
//   too: an `if` whose condition folded to a constant DISSOLVES into
//   its live arm (the node is rewritten in place into a seq wrapper
//   around that arm's chain — the dead arm is simply never emitted),
//   and a `while` over a constant false vanishes the same way; a
//   constant-TRUE while is left alone, because an infinite loop is
//   the program's right. Check runs first, so a dead arm is still
//   CHECKED — dead code must still be legal code (ncc's rule too),
//   and elimination never widens scope: a binding in a live arm still
//   dies at its brace as far as the checker is concerned.
//
//   Everything below the seam is as the earlier rungs left it.
//   Identifiers are INTERNED (a names byte-buffer plus per-symbol
//   start/len; the token's value IS the symbol index, and slots,
//   function entries and parameter tables key off it). Every token
//   records its source line, and the parser refuses bad input at its
//   expect points — "line N: error: ..." and stop, first violation
//   wins. Function entries are recorded BEFORE bodies compile, so
//   self-recursion resolves; a JMP patched over the fndefs starts
//   execution at the main statements.
//   Token encoding: kind 0 = number (val = its value), 1 = identifier
//   (val = its SYMBOL INDEX), 2 = operator/punct (val = its byte),
//   3 = '==', 4 = ':=', 5 = 'if', 6 = 'while', 7 = 'fn', 8 = 'else',
//   9 = end, 10 = string literal (val = STRING-TABLE index),
//   11 = 'print', 12 = '!=', 13 = '<=', 14 = '>=', 15 = 'len' (a
//   reserved word now, like print). Line comments (//
//   to end of line) vanish in the lexer like whitespace — the line
//   counter still ticks, so diagnostics stay honest around them.
//
//   FILE-DRIVEN, the selfhost optic: the toy also compiles a program
//   its binary does NOT embed — an audited #[caps] wrapper reads a
//   toy-language source file from disk (in-OS, off /mnt) and the SAME
//   pipeline lexes, parses, checks, folds, emits, and RUNS it. The
//   move that made it one pipeline: the lexer takes RAW BYTES plus a
//   length (*u8 + i64 — ncc's own SRC shape) instead of a str, so an
//   embedded demo hands over its .ptr/.len and a file buffer arrives
//   as itself; N cannot wrap a pointer back into a str (the ntokens
//   finding), and now nothing ever asks it to.
//   Opcodes: 0 HALT · 1 PUSH arg · 2 ADD · 3 SUB · 4 MUL · 5 DIV ·
//   6 MOD · 7 NEG · 8 LT · 9 GT · 10 EQ · 11 LOAD slot · 12 STORE slot
//   · 13 JZ target · 14 JMP target · 15 CALL target (pushes the return
//   address) · 16 RET (returns the top value) · 17 SWAP · 18 PRINT
//   (pop, write the number + newline) · 19 PRINTS (pop a string-table
//   index, write that entry + newline) · 20 STREQ (pop two table
//   indexes, byte-compare the spans, push 0/1) · 21 CONCAT (pop two
//   table indexes, append a fresh entry = both spans joined, push its
//   index) · 22 NEQ · 23 LE · 24 GE (the full comparison set, each a
//   dedicated pop-two-push-0/1 like LT/GT/EQ — on integers) ·
//   25 STRLEN (pop a table index, push its span's length). `len(s)`
//   completes the string toolkit — make (+), compare (==), measure —
//   typed str-in/int-out, and len of a literal folds to a number at
//   compile time. · 26 NOT (pop, push the logical flip: zero to one,
//   anything else to zero) — bare `!` as a factor, int-only, folding
//   over constants; the `!=` two-char arm eats its pair first, so a
//   lone '!' can only ever mean this. · 27 STRNEQ (STREQ's
//   byte-compare, pushing the flip) — string equality is symmetric
//   now: == and != both resolve over two strings. · 28 ITOS (pop an
//   integer, append its decimal image as a fresh table entry through
//   the shared cursor, push the entry's index) — how an interpolation
//   hole turns a number into text at run time; over a constant it
//   folds instead, writing the digits at compile time.
//
//   INTERPOLATION — the real lexer's hardest feature, at toy scale. A
//   double-quoted string containing a brace lexes as SEGMENTS: kind 16
//   HEAD (the text before the first hole), then the hole's expression
//   as ordinary tokens (the lexer switches to EXPRESSION mode until
//   the matching '}'), then kind 18 MID for each between-holes segment
//   or kind 17 TAIL for the text after the last — ncc's own
//   T_STR_HEAD/MID/TAIL shape, one mode flag standing in for its mode
//   stack (one level: a string literal INSIDE a hole is refused with a
//   located error, and braces in toy strings now always open a hole —
//   N's own rule, though the toy has no \{ escape). The parser then
//   DESUGARS as it parses: segments and holes join into a plain +
//   chain, each hole wrapped in an ITOS node, and the existing type
//   machinery compiles it — the checker resolves str + str to CONCAT,
//   an int hole keeps its ITOS (number to text), and a str hole's
//   ITOS REWRITES to its child in place (a string inserts as itself —
//   N's format-by-inferred-type rule, decided by the same pass that
//   decides PRINT vs PRINTS). No new machinery below the checker: the
//   sugar compiles through the sugar-free tree. And the fold pass
//   closes the story: ITOS of a constant writes its digits through
//   the shared cursor at COMPILE time, the CONCATs above collapse,
//   and a fully-constant interpolated string becomes ONE literal —
//   PUSH + PRINTS, nothing else.
//
//   THE TYPE COLUMN. Strings are real VALUES now, and the check pass
//   is what makes that safe. A double-quoted literal lexes into a
//   side STRING TABLE (sb bytes + sts/stl spans, the interned-names
//   pattern; no escapes yet — the scan runs to the next quote) and at
//   run time a string IS its table index: a strlit node lowers to a
//   plain PUSH, so strings ride the same stack, the same vars table,
//   the same STORE/LOAD as every integer. The machine cannot tell
//   them apart — which is precisely why the checker must. check_node
//   now ANNOTATES the tree: every expression node gets a type in nt
//   (0 int, 1 str), computed bottom-up; a per-symbol table (vt) gives
//   loads the type their name was bound with, and a name's type is
//   FIXED for its lifetime ("a name cannot change type" — otherwise
//   `x := 1; if c \{ x := "s"; \} print x;` would leave x's kind
//   depending on which way c went at run time). Int-only, refused
//   with located errors: arithmetic and comparisons, conditions, call
//   arguments and returns, and a body's tail value. Strings may be
//   bound, loaded, and printed. `print expr;` is ONE form — the
//   operand's inferred type picks PRINT or PRINTS at emit, which is
//   the type column doing real work: same syntax, different code,
//   decided by the checker. The VM carries the string table alongside
//   the code; PRINTS pops the index and writes the bytes through the
//   same audited boundary every other output uses.
//   And `==` over two strings is legal — and IS A DIFFERENT
//   OPERATION: the checker REWRITES the node's opcode from EQ to
//   STREQ (byte-compare the spans, push 0/1). Overload resolution at
//   toy scale: the tree records the RESOLVED operation, so the emit
//   walker stays type-blind for operators and just emits nv. Since ==
//   yields an int, `if name == "nyx" { ... }` composes with plain
//   conditions for free. Two literal strings even fold at compile
//   time (the fold pass carries the table now). `!=` resolves the
//   same way — NEQ over two strings becomes STRNEQ, the identical
//   byte-compare pushing the flipped answer, folding likewise — so
//   the equality pair is symmetric. Mixed comparisons and the
//   ordering operators on strings stay refused.
//   `+` over two strings is CONCAT — the first operator that MAKES a
//   string. The checker resolves it the same way (nv becomes opcode
//   21, and the node's type is str, so concatenations bind, print,
//   compare, and chain). At run time the VM appends a fresh table
//   entry — both spans copied to the end of the bytes buffer — and
//   pushes its index; the table's write cursors live behind a pointer
//   field (Strs.cur), seeded by the lexer and advanced by whoever
//   appends: the VM at run time, or the FOLD pass, which concatenates
//   two literals at compile time through the very same cursor — one
//   append mechanism, two moments. Runtime strings are per-program
//   transient (the next lex resets the cursors), and the toy trusts
//   its programs to fit the table, as it does every other buffer.

#[caps(syscall)]
extern syscall {
    fn write(fd: i32, buf: #[user] *u8, len: isize) -> i64 = 1
    fn sys_open(path: *u8, flags: i64, mode: i64) -> i64   = 3
    fn sys_read(fd: i64, buf: *u8, len: isize) -> i64      = 4
    fn sys_close(fd: i64) -> i64                           = 5
    fn sys_sbrk(incr: i64) -> i64                          = 7
    fn sys_exit(code: i64) -> never                        = 0
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn put(s: str) {
    write(1, s.ptr as #[user] *u8, s.len as isize);
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn alloc_words(n: i64) -> *i64 {
    sys_sbrk(n * 8) as *i64
}

#[caps(syscall)]
fn alloc_bytes(n: i64) -> *u8 {
    sys_sbrk(n) as *u8
}

// Write p[start .. start+len) — the audited boundary PRINTS uses to
// reach the string table's bytes (ntokens' put_span, over a *u8).
#[caps(syscall)]
fn bput(p: *u8, start: i64, len: i64) {
    write(1, (p as addr + start as addr) as #[user] *u8, len as isize);
}

fn is_digit(b: i64) -> bool { b >= 48 && b <= 57 }
fn is_letter(b: i64) -> bool { b >= 97 && b <= 122 }

// Scan src into the two token arrays; returns the token count. The
// last token is always the end marker (kind 9), so the parser can
// stop without carrying the count around. Identifiers intern into the
// symbol table (nb = names bytes, ss/sl = per-symbol start/len): a
// span already seen yields its existing index, a new one is appended —
// the same move ncc's vars_find/vars_add makes over its VARS table.
fn lex(p: *u8, n: i64, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, nb: *u8, ss: *i64, sl: *i64,
       st: Strs) -> i64 {
    mut i := 0;
    mut t := 0;
    mut ns := 0;                           // interned symbol count
    mut nl := 0;                           // names buffer length
    mut sc := 0;                           // string-table entry count
    mut sbl := 0;                          // string bytes-buffer length
    mut line := 1;                         // 1-based, for diagnostics (rung 2)
    mut interp := 0;                       // 1 = inside a \{...\} hole: the
                                           // lexer is in EXPRESSION mode and
                                           // '}' resumes the string
    while i < n {
        b := p[i] as i64;
        if b == 32 || b == 9 || b == 13 {  // space / tab / CR
            i += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 10 {                       // newline: the diagnostic counter
            line += 1;
            i += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 47 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 47 {
            while i < n && p[i] as i64 != 10 {
                i += 1;                    // a line comment vanishes here;
            }                              // the newline arm keeps the count
            continue;
        }
        ln[t] = line;                      // every token records its line
        if b == 34 {                       // string start: scan the segment
            if interp == 1 {               // (no escapes; a brace ALWAYS
                fail(line, "no string literals inside an interpolation");
            }                              // opens a hole now, N's rule)
            mut j := i + 1;
            while j < n && p[j] as i64 != 34 && p[j] as i64 != 123 {
                st.sb[sbl + (j - i - 1)] = p[j];
                j += 1;
            }
            st.sts[sc] = sbl;
            st.stl[sc] = j - i - 1;
            sbl += j - i - 1;
            mut kk := 10;                  // a plain literal at the quote ...
            if j < n && p[j] as i64 == 123 {
                kk = 16;                   // ... or the HEAD of an
                interp = 1;                // interpolated one at the brace:
            }                              // expression mode until '}'
            k[t] = kk; v[t] = sc; t += 1;
            sc += 1;
            i = j + 1;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 125 && interp == 1 {       // '}' closes the hole: back to
            mut j := i + 1;                // string mode for the next segment
            while j < n && p[j] as i64 != 34 && p[j] as i64 != 123 {
                st.sb[sbl + (j - i - 1)] = p[j];
                j += 1;
            }
            st.sts[sc] = sbl;
            st.stl[sc] = j - i - 1;
            sbl += j - i - 1;
            mut kk := 17;                  // the TAIL at the quote ...
            if j < n && p[j] as i64 == 123 {
                kk = 18;                   // ... or a MID: another hole opens
            }
            if kk == 17 { interp = 0; }
            k[t] = kk; v[t] = sc; t += 1;
            sc += 1;
            i = j + 1;
            continue;
        }
        if is_digit(b) {
            mut val := 0;
            while i < n && is_digit(p[i] as i64) {
                val = val * 10 + (p[i] as i64 - 48);
                i += 1;
            }
            k[t] = 0; v[t] = val; t += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if is_letter(b) {                  // keyword or identifier
            st := i;
            mut j := i;
            while j < n && is_letter(p[j] as i64) {
                j += 1;
            }
            len := j - st;
            mut kind := 1;
            if len == 2 && p[st] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 102 {
                kind = 5;                  // if
            }
            if len == 2 && p[st] as i64 == 102 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 110 {
                kind = 7;                  // fn
            }
            if len == 5 && p[st] as i64 == 119 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 104
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 108
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 101 {
                kind = 6;                  // while
            }
            if len == 4 && p[st] as i64 == 101 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 108
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 115 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 101 {
                kind = 8;                  // else
            }
            if len == 5 && p[st] as i64 == 112 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 114
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 105 && p[st + 3] as i64 == 110
                && p[st + 4] as i64 == 116 {
                kind = 11;                 // print
            }
            if len == 3 && p[st] as i64 == 108 && p[st + 1] as i64 == 101
                && p[st + 2] as i64 == 110 {
                kind = 15;                 // len (a reserved word now)
            }
            mut val := 0;
            if kind == 1 {                 // intern: look the span up ...
                mut idx := -1;
                mut s := 0;
                while s < ns {
                    if sl[s] == len {
                        mut eq := true;
                        mut q := 0;
                        while q < len {
                            if nb[ss[s] + q] as i64 != p[st + q] as i64 {
                                eq = false;
                            }
                            q += 1;
                        }
                        if eq && idx < 0 { idx = s; }
                    }
                    s += 1;
                }
                if idx < 0 {               // ... or append a new symbol
                    ss[ns] = nl;
                    sl[ns] = len;
                    mut q := 0;
                    while q < len {
                        nb[nl + q] = p[st + q];
                        q += 1;
                    }
                    nl += len;
                    idx = ns;
                    ns += 1;
                }
                val = idx;
            }
            k[t] = kind; v[t] = val; t += 1;
            i = j;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 58 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 4; v[t] = 0; t += 1;    // :=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 61 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 3; v[t] = 0; t += 1;    // ==
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 33 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 12; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // !=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 60 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 13; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // <=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        if b == 62 && i + 1 < n && p[i + 1] as i64 == 61 {
            k[t] = 14; v[t] = 0; t += 1;   // >=
            i += 2;
            continue;
        }
        k[t] = 2; v[t] = b; t += 1;        // operator / punct, by byte
        i += 1;
    }
    k[t] = 9; v[t] = 0; ln[t] = line;      // end marker
    st.cur[0] = sc;                        // seed the table's write cursors:
    st.cur[1] = sbl;                       // appends (fold or VM) start here
    t + 1
}

// Rung 2 of the selfhost road: the toy REFUSES bad input instead of
// wandering into undefined behavior. First error wins; the message
// carries the token's source line. (It exits with status 0 so the test
// harness — which treats a non-zero exit as an example crash — stays
// strict for everything else; a real compiler would exit 1.)
#[caps(syscall)]
fn fail(line: i64, msg: str) -> never {
    put("line {line}: error: {msg}\n");
    sys_exit(0);
}

// The expression tree, as parallel i64 arrays in one by-value struct:
// nk = node kind, nl/nr = child indexes, nv = the node's value (a
// number, a slot, an opcode, or a function symbol), na = the flat
// argument table (four root indexes per call node, na[idx*4 + j]),
// nline = the node's source line, so the check pass can locate blame.
struct Ast { nk: *i64, nl: *i64, nr: *i64, nv: *i64, na: *i64, nline: *i64,
             nt: *i64 }

// The string table, one by-value bundle: literal bytes + per-entry
// start/len spans + the WRITE CURSORS (cur[0] = entry count, cur[1] =
// bytes used) behind a pointer, so every by-value copy shares them —
// the lexer seeds them, and the fold pass or the VM advances them
// when a CONCAT appends. (Bundling matters practically too: N caps
// functions at 16 parameters, and the toy's plumbing was at the door.)
struct Strs { sb: *u8, sts: *i64, stl: *i64, cur: *i64 }

// The checker's environment: the bound-name list (which symbols are
// live, bv + a threaded count) and the per-symbol type table (vt —
// what kind of value each name holds, 0 int / 1 str). The flat
// runtime table cannot tell an integer from a string index; the
// checker can, and this is where it keeps the knowledge.
struct Scope { bv: *i64, vt: *i64 }

// The outer skeleton's cursors: pos walks tokens, cp walks code. The
// bound-name count no longer threads through parsing — scope lives in
// the check walk, where it belongs.
struct EP { pos: i64, cp: i64 }

// The build-phase cursors: pos walks tokens, nn is the next free node,
// root is the subtree this rule just built.
struct NP { pos: i64, nn: i64, root: i64 }

fn emit1(code: *i64, cp: i64, op: i64) -> i64 {
    code[cp] = op;
    cp + 1
}

fn emit2(code: *i64, cp: i64, op: i64, arg: i64) -> i64 {
    code[cp] = op;
    code[cp + 1] = arg;
    cp + 2
}

// factor := number | name '(' cmp { ',' cmp } ')' | name
//         | '-' factor | '(' cmp ')'
//   Phase one only BUILDS: every arm appends a node and returns its
//   index — not a single opcode is emitted on this side of the seam.
//   The call arm allocates its node FIRST so the argument roots can
//   fill its slice of the arg table in place; and with the whole call
//   in hand as data, the parser finally checks it — the argument count
//   must match the callee's parameter count.
fn ast_factor(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if k[pos] == 0 {                       // number
        a.nk[nn] = 0; a.nl[nn] = 0; a.nr[nn] = 0; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 10 {                      // string literal: a value now —
        a.nk[nn] = 10; a.nl[nn] = -1;      // its table index, PUSHed like
        a.nr[nn] = -1; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];  // any number; the type column
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];             // keeps the kinds apart
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 16 {                      // interpolated string: HEAD, then
        a.nk[nn] = 10; a.nl[nn] = -1;      // hole + segment pairs until the
        a.nr[nn] = -1; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];  // TAIL. Desugared AS PARSED into
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];             // plain ADD nodes over the pieces
        mut cur := nn;                     // — each hole wrapped in an ITOS
        mut n2 := nn + 1;                  // node — and the CHECKER later
        mut p := pos + 1;                  // resolves str + str to CONCAT:
        mut going := true;                 // the sugar compiles through the
        while going {                      // sugar-free tree.
            r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, p, n2);
            w := r.nn;                     // the hole's expression, wrapped
            a.nk[w] = 13; a.nl[w] = r.root; a.nr[w] = -1; a.nv[w] = 0;
            a.nline[w] = ln[p];
            j1 := w + 1;                   // joined onto the chain so far
            a.nk[j1] = 2; a.nl[j1] = cur; a.nr[j1] = w; a.nv[j1] = 2;
            a.nline[j1] = ln[p];
            if !(k[r.pos] == 17 || k[r.pos] == 18) {
                fail(ln[r.pos], "unterminated interpolation");
            }
            s2 := j1 + 1;                  // the segment after the hole
            a.nk[s2] = 10; a.nl[s2] = -1; a.nr[s2] = -1; a.nv[s2] = v[r.pos];
            a.nline[s2] = ln[r.pos];
            j2 := s2 + 1;                  // joined on; a MID keeps going
            a.nk[j2] = 2; a.nl[j2] = j1; a.nr[j2] = s2; a.nv[j2] = 2;
            a.nline[j2] = ln[r.pos];
            cur = j2;
            going = k[r.pos] == 18;
            p = r.pos + 1;
            n2 = j2 + 1;
        }
        return NP{ pos: p, nn: n2, root: cur };
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40 {
        f := v[pos];                       // name '(' — a call
        idx := nn;                         // the call node, made first
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        mut n2 := nn + 1;
        mut p := pos + 2;
        mut cnt := 0;
        mut more := !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 41);
        while more {                       // comma-separated argument trees
            more = false;
            r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, p, n2);
            if cnt == 4 {                  // the arg table holds four
                fail(ln[p], "wrong number of arguments");
            }
            a.na[idx * 4 + cnt] = r.root;
            cnt += 1;
            p = r.pos;
            n2 = r.nn;
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 44 {   // ',' — another argument
                p += 1;
                more = true;
            }
        }
        if !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 41) {
            fail(ln[p], "expected ')' after the arguments");
        }
        if cnt != fpc[f] {                 // the first check that reads a call
            fail(ln[p], "wrong number of arguments");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 4; a.nl[idx] = cnt; a.nr[idx] = 0; a.nv[idx] = f;
        return NP{ pos: p + 1, nn: n2, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 1 {                       // name read
        a.nk[nn] = 1; a.nl[nn] = 0; a.nr[nn] = 0; a.nv[nn] = v[pos];
        a.nline[nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: pos + 1, nn: nn + 1, root: nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 45 {       // unary minus
        r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn);
        a.nk[r.nn] = 3; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = 0; a.nv[r.nn] = 0;
        a.nline[r.nn] = ln[pos];
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 33 {       // logical not (a bare '!' — the
        r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn);   // != arm ate '!='
        a.nk[r.nn] = 12; a.nl[r.nn] = r.root; a.nr[r.nn] = -1; a.nv[r.nn] = 0;
        a.nline[r.nn] = ln[pos];           // before this could ever see it)
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    if k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 40 {       // '(' — mutual recursion
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 41) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ')'");
        }
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: r.root };
    }
    if k[pos] == 15 {                      // len ( cmp ) — the measuring
        idx := nn;                         // builtin: str in, int out
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        if !(k[pos + 1] == 2 && v[pos + 1] == 40) {
            fail(ln[pos], "expected '(' after len");
        }
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 2, nn + 1);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 41) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ')'");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 11; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    fail(ln[pos], "expected an expression");
    NP{ pos: pos, nn: nn, root: 0 }        // unreachable; keeps the lowering total
}

// term := factor (('*' | '/' | '%') factor)*
//   The operator tag stored in a binop node IS its VM opcode — one
//   name for one thing, so the walker emits nv directly.
fn ast_term(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    mut r := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos, nn);
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && (v[r.pos] == 42 || v[r.pos] == 47 || v[r.pos] == 37) {
            op := v[r.pos];
            opl := ln[r.pos];
            n := ast_factor(k, v, ln, fpc, a, r.pos + 1, r.nn);
            mut oc := 4;                   // MUL
            if op == 47 { oc = 5; }        // DIV
            if op == 37 { oc = 6; }        // MOD
            a.nk[n.nn] = 2; a.nl[n.nn] = r.root; a.nr[n.nn] = n.root; a.nv[n.nn] = oc;
            a.nline[n.nn] = opl;
            r = NP{ pos: n.pos, nn: n.nn + 1, root: n.nn };
            going = true;
        }
    }
    r
}

// sum := term (('+' | '-') term)*
fn ast_sum(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    mut r := ast_term(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos, nn);
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[r.pos] == 2 && (v[r.pos] == 43 || v[r.pos] == 45) {
            op := v[r.pos];
            opl := ln[r.pos];
            n := ast_term(k, v, ln, fpc, a, r.pos + 1, r.nn);
            mut oc := 2;                   // ADD
            if op == 45 { oc = 3; }        // SUB
            a.nk[n.nn] = 2; a.nl[n.nn] = r.root; a.nr[n.nn] = n.root; a.nv[n.nn] = oc;
            a.nline[n.nn] = opl;
            r = NP{ pos: n.pos, nn: n.nn + 1, root: n.nn };
            going = true;
        }
    }
    r
}

// cmp := sum [('<' | '>' | '==' | '!=' | '<=' | '>=') sum]
//   — lowest tier, pushes 0 or 1: the full comparison set.
fn ast_cmp(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    l := ast_sum(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos, nn);
    two := k[l.pos] == 3 || k[l.pos] == 12 || k[l.pos] == 13 || k[l.pos] == 14;
    one := k[l.pos] == 2 && (v[l.pos] == 60 || v[l.pos] == 62);
    if two || one {
        mut oc := 10;                      // EQ
        if one && v[l.pos] == 60 { oc = 8; }    // LT
        if one && v[l.pos] == 62 { oc = 9; }    // GT
        if k[l.pos] == 12 { oc = 22; }          // NEQ
        if k[l.pos] == 13 { oc = 23; }          // LE
        if k[l.pos] == 14 { oc = 24; }          // GE
        opl := ln[l.pos];
        r := ast_sum(k, v, ln, fpc, a, l.pos + 1, l.nn);
        a.nk[r.nn] = 2; a.nl[r.nn] = l.root; a.nr[r.nn] = r.root; a.nv[r.nn] = oc;
        a.nline[r.nn] = opl;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos, nn: r.nn + 1, root: r.nn };
    }
    l
}

// THE CHECK PASS — the first consumer of the tree that is not the
// emitter, and the reason the tree exists: it looks at a whole
// expression while NO code for it has been generated yet, and emits
// nothing itself. Two duties now. SCOPE: every load must name a BOUND
// variable — a parameter of the enclosing function, or a name a `:=`
// statement already bound in this scope (env.bv holds their symbol
// indexes, bc many of them are live). TYPES: the walk ANNOTATES the
// tree bottom-up — every node's kind of value lands in nt (0 int,
// 1 str): numbers and calls are int, literals are str, a load carries
// the type its name was bound with (env.vt), and the int-only
// operations refuse strings with located errors. The predicted shape
// held: rules accrete on this one walk — which is exactly how ncc's
// check_expr grew.
fn check_node(a: Ast, env: Scope, bc: i64, idx: i64) {
    if a.nk[idx] == 0 {                    // number: int, by construction
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 10 {                   // string literal: str
        a.nt[idx] = 1;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 1 {                    // load: bound, and typed as bound
        mut ok := false;
        mut i := 0;
        while i < bc {
            if env.bv[i] == a.nv[idx] { ok = true; }
            i += 1;
        }
        if !ok {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "unknown variable");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = env.vt[a.nv[idx]];
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: children first, then the
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]); // types resolve the OPERATION
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
        lt := a.nt[a.nl[idx]];
        rt := a.nt[a.nr[idx]];
        mut resolved := false;
        mut ty := 0;
        if a.nv[idx] == 10 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 20;                // == over two strings IS STREQ:
            resolved = true;               // the checker rewrites the opcode,
        }                                  // emit stays type-blind
        if a.nv[idx] == 22 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 27;                // != over two strings IS STRNEQ —
            resolved = true;               // the same rewrite, the answer
        }                                  // flipped; equality symmetric now
        if a.nv[idx] == 2 && lt == 1 && rt == 1 {
            a.nv[idx] = 21;                // + over two strings IS CONCAT —
            resolved = true;               // the first operator that MAKES a
            ty = 1;                        // string, so the result is str
        }
        if !resolved && (lt == 1 || rt == 1) {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot use a string in arithmetic or comparison");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = ty;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // unary minus: an int operand
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot negate a string");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len: measures a STRING, is int
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] != 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "len takes a string");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // logical not: int in, int out
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "! takes an integer");
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // interpolation hole: an integer
        c := a.nl[idx];                    // becomes text (ITOS stays); a
        check_node(a, env, bc, c);         // string inserts as ITSELF — the
        if a.nt[c] == 1 {                  // node REWRITES to its child (the
            a.nk[idx] = a.nk[c];           // checker's in-place move again),
            a.nl[idx] = a.nl[c];           // and the surrounding ADD chain
            a.nr[idx] = a.nr[c];           // then resolves to CONCAT on its
            a.nv[idx] = a.nv[c];           // own — N's by-inferred-type
            a.na[idx * 4] = a.na[c * 4];   // interpolation rule, at toy
            a.na[idx * 4 + 1] = a.na[c * 4 + 1];   // scale
            a.na[idx * 4 + 2] = a.na[c * 4 + 2];
            a.na[idx * 4 + 3] = a.na[c * 4 + 3];
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 1;                     // either way the hole yields str
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 4 {                    // call: int arguments, int result
        mut q := 0;
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            check_node(a, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            if a.nt[a.na[idx * 4 + q]] == 1 {
                fail(a.nline[idx], "cannot pass a string to a function");
            }
            q += 1;
        }
        a.nt[idx] = 0;
    }
}

// THE FIRST TRANSFORM: constant folding, in place. Children first, so
// nested constant expressions collapse bottom-up ((2*3)+4 folds the
// MUL, sees two numbers at the ADD, folds again). A rewritten node
// simply changes kind to 0 with the computed value — its old children
// stay allocated but unreachable, which a transient per-expression
// tree can afford. DIV/MOD by a literal zero stay unfolded (zerodiv):
// the compiler must not crash computing what the program would.
fn fold_node(a: Ast, st: Strs, idx: i64) {
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: fold children, then try
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        fold_node(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
        l := a.nl[idx];
        r := a.nr[idx];
        if a.nk[l] == 0 && a.nk[r] == 0 {
            x := a.nv[l];
            y := a.nv[r];
            oc := a.nv[idx];
            zerodiv := (oc == 5 || oc == 6) && y == 0;
            if !zerodiv {
                mut val := 0;
                if oc == 2 { val = x + y; }
                if oc == 3 { val = x - y; }
                if oc == 4 { val = x * y; }
                if oc == 5 { val = x / y; }
                if oc == 6 { val = x % y; }
                if oc == 8 { if x < y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 9 { if x > y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 10 { if x == y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 22 { if x != y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 23 { if x <= y { val = 1; } }
                if oc == 24 { if x >= y { val = 1; } }
                a.nk[idx] = 0;             // the operation happened NOW
                a.nv[idx] = val;
            }
        }
        if (a.nv[idx] == 20 || a.nv[idx] == 27) && a.nk[l] == 10 && a.nk[r] == 10 {
            mut eq := 0;                   // two literals: compare them NOW
            if st.stl[a.nv[l]] == st.stl[a.nv[r]] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[a.nv[l]] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[l]] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[r]] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            if a.nv[idx] == 27 {           // != is the same compare with
                eq = 1 - eq;               // the answer flipped
            }
            a.nk[idx] = 0;                 // the comparison happened at
            a.nv[idx] = eq;                // compile time
        }
        if a.nv[idx] == 21 && a.nk[l] == 10 && a.nk[r] == 10 {
            base := st.cur[1];             // two literals: append the joined
            mut q := 0;                    // entry NOW, through the same
            while q < st.stl[a.nv[l]] {    // cursor the VM would use
                st.sb[base + q] = st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[l]] + q];
                q += 1;
            }
            mut w := 0;
            while w < st.stl[a.nv[r]] {
                st.sb[base + st.stl[a.nv[l]] + w] = st.sb[st.sts[a.nv[r]] + w];
                w += 1;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = st.stl[a.nv[l]] + st.stl[a.nv[r]];
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + st.stl[ni];
            a.nk[idx] = 10;                // the node becomes that literal
            a.nv[idx] = ni;
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // negation of a folded number
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = -a.nv[c];
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len of a literal: its span
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // length is known right now
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 10 {
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = st.stl[a.nv[c]];
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // not of a folded number
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        c := a.nl[idx];
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {
            mut nv := 0;
            if a.nv[c] == 0 { nv = 1; }
            a.nk[idx] = 0;
            a.nv[idx] = nv;
        }
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // itos of a folded number: the
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);       // digits are known right now —
        c := a.nl[idx];                    // append the entry at compile
        if a.nk[c] == 0 {                  // time through the shared cursor
            mut x := a.nv[c];
            base := st.cur[1];
            mut len := 0;
            if x < 0 {
                st.sb[base] = 45 as u8;
                len = 1;
                x = -x;
            }
            mut m := 1;                    // digits forward by place value
            while x / m >= 10 { m = m * 10; }
            while m > 0 {
                st.sb[base + len] = (48 + (x / m) % 10) as u8;
                len += 1;
                m = m / 10;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = len;
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + len;
            a.nk[idx] = 10;                // the node becomes that literal —
            a.nv[idx] = ni;                // and the CONCAT folds above can
        }                                  // then collapse the whole string
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 4 {                    // call: arguments fold, calls never
        mut q := 0;
        while q < a.nl[idx] {
            fold_node(a, st, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
            q += 1;
        }
    }
}

// Phase two: the postorder walk. Children first, the node's own
// operation last — and the walker emits byte-for-byte what the fused
// parser-emitter used to, no matter how the tree was built. The
// caller-save discipline lives HERE now: parse decided WHAT (a call of
// f with these argument trees), this decides HOW — save the callee's
// slots in order, evaluate all the argument trees, bind in reverse
// (pops hand back the last argument first), CALL, then restore each
// saved slot in reverse with SWAP + STORE beneath the returned value.
fn emit_node(a: Ast, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, code: *i64, cp: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if a.nk[idx] == 0 {                    // number: PUSH its value
        return emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 1 {                    // name read: LOAD its slot
        return emit2(code, cp, 11, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 10 {                   // string literal: at run time a
        return emit2(code, cp, 1, a.nv[idx]);   // string IS its table index
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 2 {                    // binop: lhs, rhs, then the opcode
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        c2 := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nr[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c2, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 3 {                    // unary minus: operand, then NEG
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 7);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 11 {                   // len: operand, then STRLEN
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 25);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 12 {                   // logical not: operand, then NOT
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 26);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 13 {                   // itos: operand, then ITOS
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit1(code, c, 28);
    }
    f := a.nv[idx];                        // call
    cnt := a.nl[idx];
    mut c := cp;
    mut j := 0;
    while j < cnt {                        // save the callee's slots, in order
        c = emit2(code, c, 11, fp[f * 4 + j]);
        j += 1;
    }
    mut q := 0;
    while q < cnt {                        // all argument trees, before any binds
        c = emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.na[idx * 4 + q]);
        q += 1;
    }
    mut w := cnt - 1;
    while w > -1 {                         // bind in reverse: pops give the
        c = emit2(code, c, 12, fp[f * 4 + w]);   // last argument first
        w -= 1;
    }
    c = emit2(code, c, 15, fe[f]);         // CALL the entry
    mut u := cnt - 1;
    while u > -1 {                         // restore in reverse, beneath the
        c = emit1(code, c, 17);            // returned value: SWAP + STORE
        c = emit2(code, c, 12, fp[f * 4 + u]);
        u -= 1;
    }
    c
}

// A statement begins with `if`, `while`, `print`, or a name + ':='.
fn is_stmt_start(k: *i64, pos: i64) -> bool {
    k[pos] == 5 || k[pos] == 6 || k[pos] == 11 || (k[pos] == 1 && k[pos + 1] == 4)
}

// stmt := name ':=' cmp ';' | 'if' cmp block [ 'else' block ]
//       | 'while' cmp block
//   Statements BUILD nodes now, like every expression rule above: the
//   statement node is allocated first (the call-node trick), its
//   pieces land in nl/nr/na, and not one opcode is emitted here. The
//   jump-patching craft lives in emit_stmt.
fn ast_stmt(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if k[pos] == 5 {                       // if cmp block [else block]
        idx := nn;
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        c := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        t := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, a, c.pos, c.nn);
        a.nk[idx] = 6; a.nl[idx] = c.root; a.nr[idx] = t.root; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        a.na[idx * 4] = -1;                // no else (yet)
        if k[t.pos] == 8 {
            if k[t.pos + 1] == 5 {         // else IF: the chain. The else
                e := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, a, t.pos + 1, t.nn);
                a.na[idx * 4] = e.root;    // arm IS the next if statement —
                return NP{ pos: e.pos, nn: e.nn, root: idx };
            }                              // no walker cares: the else slot
            e := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, a, t.pos + 1, t.nn);
            a.na[idx * 4] = e.root;        // held any statement all along
            return NP{ pos: e.pos, nn: e.nn, root: idx };
        }
        return NP{ pos: t.pos, nn: t.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 6 {                       // while cmp block
        idx := nn;
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
        c := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        b := ast_block(k, v, ln, fpc, a, c.pos, c.nn);
        a.nk[idx] = 7; a.nl[idx] = c.root; a.nr[idx] = b.root; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: b.pos, nn: b.nn, root: idx };
    }
    if k[pos] == 11 {                      // print cmp ; — ONE form: the
        idx := nn;                         // operand's TYPE picks the
        a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];            // opcode at emit
        r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 1, nn + 1);
        if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
            fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after print");
        }
        a.nk[idx] = 9; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = 0;
        return NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx };
    }
    idx := nn;                             // name ':=' cmp ';'
    a.nline[idx] = ln[pos];
    slot := v[pos];
    r := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, pos + 2, nn + 1);
    if !(k[r.pos] == 2 && v[r.pos] == 59) {
        fail(ln[r.pos], "expected ';' after the binding");
    }
    a.nk[idx] = 5; a.nl[idx] = r.root; a.nr[idx] = -1; a.nv[idx] = slot;
    NP{ pos: r.pos + 1, nn: r.nn, root: idx }
}

// block := '{' { stmt } '}'   — a seq CHAIN in the node arrays: each
//   link is a seq node (nl = the statement, nr = the next link, -1
//   ends it), patched forward as statements arrive. An empty block is
//   root -1. Scope needs no bookkeeping here any more — the check walk
//   owns it.
fn ast_block(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, pos: i64, nn: i64) -> NP {
    if !(k[pos] == 2 && v[pos] == 123) {
        fail(ln[pos], "expected '\{'");
    }
    mut p := pos + 1;                      // skip '{'
    mut n2 := nn;
    mut head := -1;
    mut prev := -1;
    mut more := true;
    while more {
        more = false;
        if is_stmt_start(k, p) {
            s := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, a, p, n2);
            seq := s.nn;                   // the chain link
            a.nk[seq] = 8; a.nl[seq] = s.root; a.nr[seq] = -1; a.nv[seq] = 0;
            a.nline[seq] = a.nline[s.root];
            if prev > -1 { a.nr[prev] = seq; } else { head = seq; }
            prev = seq;
            n2 = s.nn + 1;
            p = s.pos;
            more = true;
        }
    }
    if !(k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 125) {
        fail(ln[p], "expected '\}' or a statement");
    }
    NP{ pos: p + 1, nn: n2, root: head }   // skip '}'
}

// The statement half of the CHECK pass: a seq chain threads the bound
// count forward and returns the new count; a bind adds its name only
// AFTER its right-hand side checks (`x := x + 1` cannot self-feed);
// if arms and while bodies check with the count they were given and
// this fn hands back the ORIGINAL — branch names die with the branch.
fn check_stmt(a: Ast, env: Scope, bc: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if idx < 0 {                           // an empty chain binds nothing
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 {                    // seq: this statement, then the rest
        b := check_stmt(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        return check_stmt(a, env, b, a.nr[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 {                    // bind: rhs first, THEN the name.
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        slot := a.nv[idx];                 // A name's type is FIXED for its
        mut seen := false;                 // lifetime: rebinding must keep
        mut i := 0;                        // it, or the flat runtime table
        while i < bc {                     // could hold either kind at once
            if env.bv[i] == slot { seen = true; }
            i += 1;
        }
        if seen && env.vt[slot] != a.nt[a.nl[idx]] {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a name cannot change type");
        }
        env.vt[slot] = a.nt[a.nl[idx]];
        env.bv[bc] = slot;
        return bc + 1;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {                    // if: an int condition; both arms
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {
            fail(a.nline[idx], "a condition must be an integer");
        }
        check_stmt(a, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
        check_stmt(a, env, bc, a.na[idx * 4]);
        return bc;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 {                    // print: either type is welcome —
        check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]); // the operand's nt picks the
        return bc;                         // opcode at emit
    }
    check_node(a, env, bc, a.nl[idx]);     // while: int cond + body, restored
    if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {
        fail(a.nline[idx], "a condition must be an integer");
    }
    check_stmt(a, env, bc, a.nr[idx]);
    bc
}

// The statement half of the FOLD pass: recurse to every expression
// root and fold it — and rewrite STATEMENTS whose shape the folded
// constants decide. An if over a constant condition dissolves into
// its live arm: the node is rewritten in place into a seq wrapper
// around that arm's chain (a seq in statement position nests fine —
// every walker handles kind 8 and -1 generically, so no other pass
// changed at all). The dead arm is left where it stands, unreachable
// from the tree — never emitted. A while over constant FALSE becomes
// an empty wrapper the same way; over constant TRUE it is left alone
// (an infinite loop is the program's right), body still folding.
fn fold_stmt(a: Ast, st: Strs, idx: i64) {
    if idx < 0 {
        return;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 { fold_stmt(a, st, a.nl[idx]); fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 { fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]); }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nk[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {          // constant condition: dead-arm
            mut live := a.nr[idx];         // elimination — nonzero keeps
            if a.nv[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {      // then, zero keeps else
                live = a.na[idx * 4];
            }
            fold_stmt(a, st, live);
            a.nk[idx] = 8;                 // the if dissolves into a seq
            a.nl[idx] = live;              // wrapper around the live arm
            a.nr[idx] = -1;                // (or around nothing at all)
            return;
        }
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.na[idx * 4]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 7 {
        fold_node(a, st, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nk[a.nl[idx]] == 0 && a.nv[a.nl[idx]] == 0 {
            a.nk[idx] = 8;                 // while false: it never runs —
            a.nl[idx] = -1;                // an empty wrapper remains
            a.nr[idx] = -1;
            return;
        }
        fold_stmt(a, st, a.nr[idx]);
    }
}

// The statement half of the EMIT walk — the jump-patching craft moved
// here from the old emitting parser, unchanged in shape: the target is
// not known until the arm has been emitted, so JZ takes a placeholder
// and a later index store patches it; `while` emits its back-JMP and
// the JZ patch lands past it; `else` is the classic diamond.
fn emit_stmt(a: Ast, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, code: *i64, cp: i64, idx: i64) -> i64 {
    if idx < 0 {                           // an empty chain emits nothing
        return cp;
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 8 {                    // seq: statement, then the rest
        c := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c, a.nr[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 5 {                    // bind: rhs, then STORE its slot
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        return emit2(code, c, 12, a.nv[idx]);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 9 {                    // print: the operand's TYPE picks
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        if a.nt[a.nl[idx]] == 1 {          // the opcode — the type column
            return emit1(code, c, 19);     // doing real work
        }
        return emit1(code, c, 18);
    }
    if a.nk[idx] == 6 {                    // if / else
        c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
        hole := c + 1;                     // the JZ's target word
        t := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, emit2(code, c, 13, 0), a.nr[idx]);
        if a.na[idx * 4] > -1 {            // else: the diamond —
            hole2 := t + 1;                // the then-arm hops past the else,
            c2 := emit2(code, t, 14, 0);
            code[hole] = c2;               // false lands at the else entry,
            e := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, c2, a.na[idx * 4]);
            code[hole2] = e;               // and the hop lands past it all
            return e;
        }
        code[hole] = t;                    // no else: skip the arm when false
        return t;
    }
    top := cp;                             // while: cond, JZ out, body, back
    c := emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, cp, a.nl[idx]);
    hole := c + 1;
    t := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, emit2(code, c, 13, 0), a.nr[idx]);
    e := emit2(code, t, 14, top);          // back to the condition
    code[hole] = e;                        // exit lands past the back-jump
    e
}

// Build one body — { stmt } cmp — as a single fresh tree (the node
// cursor restarts at zero: trees are transient per body now, not per
// expression), then run THE PASSES over it in order: check (seeded
// with nseed already-bound names sitting in bv), fold, emit. This is
// the compiler's whole shape in eight lines — ncc's parse → check →
// gen, at toy scale, shared by function bodies and the main program.
fn gen_body(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, st: Strs, nseed: i64, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    mut p := pos;
    mut n2 := 0;
    mut head := -1;
    mut prev := -1;
    mut more := true;
    while more {                           // the statement chain, if any
        more = false;
        if is_stmt_start(k, p) {
            s := ast_stmt(k, v, ln, fpc, a, p, n2);
            seq := s.nn;
            a.nk[seq] = 8; a.nl[seq] = s.root; a.nr[seq] = -1; a.nv[seq] = 0;
            a.nline[seq] = a.nline[s.root];
            if prev > -1 { a.nr[prev] = seq; } else { head = seq; }
            prev = seq;
            n2 = s.nn + 1;
            p = s.pos;
            more = true;
        }
    }
    t := ast_cmp(k, v, ln, fpc, a, p, n2); // the tail is the body's value
    b := check_stmt(a, env, nseed, head);  // pass 1: check
    check_node(a, env, b, t.root);
    if a.nt[t.root] == 1 {                 // values and returns stay int —
        fail(a.nline[t.root], "a body's value must be an integer");
    }
    fold_stmt(a, st, head);                    // pass 2: fold
    fold_node(a, st, t.root);
    c := emit_stmt(a, fe, fp, code, cp, head);   // pass 3: emit
    EP{ pos: t.pos, cp: emit_node(a, fe, fp, code, c, t.root) }
}

// fndef := 'fn' name '(' name { ',' name } ')' '{' { stmt } cmp '}'
//   The entry address is recorded BEFORE the body compiles, so a
//   function may call itself; up to four comma-separated parameters
//   record their slots in fp[f*4 + j] with the count in fpc[f]; the
//   body ends in RET, which returns its tail value.
//   A body's scope starts FRESH: its parameters are seeded into bv as
//   the first bound names (index 0 up — all fndefs precede the main
//   statements, so nothing is trampled), and everything else it reads
//   must be bound by its own statements. The flat runtime table would
//   let a body read main's names, but that is caller-save's private
//   business — the checker enforces the lexical story.
fn parse_fndef(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, st: Strs, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    f := v[pos + 1];
    fe[f] = cp;
    mut p := pos + 3;                      // fn +0, name +1, '(' +2, params...
    mut j := 0;
    mut going := true;
    while going {
        going = false;
        if k[p] == 1 {
            fp[f * 4 + j] = v[p];
            env.bv[j] = v[p];              // a parameter is born bound,
            env.vt[v[p]] = 0;              // and it is an integer
            j += 1;
            p += 1;
            if k[p] == 2 && v[p] == 44 {   // ',' — another parameter follows
                p += 1;
                going = true;
            }
        }
    }
    fpc[f] = j;
    t := gen_body(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, a, env, st, j, p + 2, code, cp);
    EP{ pos: t.pos + 1, cp: emit1(code, t.cp, 16) }   // skip '}', RET
}

// program := { fndef } { stmt } cmp
//   If any functions exist, a JMP with a placeholder target is emitted
//   first and patched to hop over their bodies — execution starts at
//   the main statements, functions are entered only by CALL.
fn parse_program(k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, st: Strs, pos: i64, code: *i64, cp: i64) -> EP {
    mut p := pos;
    mut c := cp;
    if k[p] == 7 {
        c = emit2(code, c, 14, 0);
        hole := c - 1;                     // the hop-over JMP's target
        mut fns := true;
        while fns {
            fns = false;
            if k[p] == 7 {
                r := parse_fndef(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, a, env, st, p, code, c);
                p = r.pos;
                c = r.cp;
                fns = true;
            }
        }
        code[hole] = c;                    // main statements start here
    }
    t := gen_body(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, a, env, st, 0, p, code, c);
    if k[t.pos] != 9 {                     // main's scope starts empty
        fail(ln[t.pos], "unexpected input after the tail expression");
    }
    t
}

// The stack machine: arithmetic, comparisons, a variable table, jumps,
// calls — and output. CALL pushes the return address and jumps; RET
// consumes it beneath the return value; SWAP serves the caller-save
// restore. The string table (sb/sts/stl) rides along so PRINTS can
// reach its bytes.
fn run(prog: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64, st: Strs) -> i64 {
    mut pc := 0;
    mut sp := 0;
    mut running := true;
    while running {
        op := prog[pc];
        if op == 0 { running = false; }
        if op == 1 { stk[sp] = prog[pc + 1]; sp += 1; pc += 2; }
        if op == 2 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] + stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 3 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] - stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 4 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] * stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 5 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] / stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 6 { stk[sp - 2] = stk[sp - 2] % stk[sp - 1]; sp -= 1; pc += 1; }
        if op == 7 { stk[sp - 1] = -stk[sp - 1]; pc += 1; }
        if op == 8 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] < stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 9 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] > stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 10 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] == stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 11 { stk[sp] = vars[prog[pc + 1]]; sp += 1; pc += 2; }
        if op == 12 { sp -= 1; vars[prog[pc + 1]] = stk[sp]; pc += 2; }
        if op == 13 {
            sp -= 1;
            if stk[sp] == 0 { pc = prog[pc + 1]; } else { pc += 2; }
        }
        if op == 14 { pc = prog[pc + 1]; }
        if op == 15 { stk[sp] = pc + 2; sp += 1; pc = prog[pc + 1]; }
        if op == 16 {
            rv := stk[sp - 1];             // the body's tail value
            pc = stk[sp - 2];              // the pushed return address
            stk[sp - 2] = rv;
            sp -= 1;
        }
        if op == 17 {
            t := stk[sp - 1];
            stk[sp - 1] = stk[sp - 2];
            stk[sp - 2] = t;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 18 {                      // PRINT: the popped number
            sp -= 1;
            x := stk[sp];
            put("{x}\n");
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 19 {                      // PRINTS: pop a table index and
            sp -= 1;                       // write that entry — strings are
            s := stk[sp];                  // stack values like any other
            bput(st.sb, st.sts[s], st.stl[s]);
            put("\n");
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 20 {                      // STREQ: pop two table indexes,
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // byte-compare the spans
            x := stk[sp - 2];
            mut eq := 0;
            if st.stl[x] == st.stl[y] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[x] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[x] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[y] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            stk[sp - 2] = eq;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 21 {                      // CONCAT: pop two indexes, append
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // a fresh entry = x's bytes then
            x := stk[sp - 2];              // y's, push the new index
            base := st.cur[1];
            mut q := 0;
            while q < st.stl[x] {
                st.sb[base + q] = st.sb[st.sts[x] + q];
                q += 1;
            }
            mut w := 0;
            while w < st.stl[y] {
                st.sb[base + st.stl[x] + w] = st.sb[st.sts[y] + w];
                w += 1;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = st.stl[x] + st.stl[y];
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + st.stl[ni];
            stk[sp - 2] = ni;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 22 {                      // NEQ / LE / GE: the rest of the
            mut t := 0;                    // comparison set, LT/GT/EQ-shaped
            if stk[sp - 2] != stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 23 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] <= stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 24 {
            mut t := 0;
            if stk[sp - 2] >= stk[sp - 1] { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 2] = t; sp -= 1; pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 25 {                      // STRLEN: a table index becomes
            stk[sp - 1] = st.stl[stk[sp - 1]];   // its span's length
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 26 {                      // NOT: zero flips to one and
            mut t := 0;                    // anything else to zero
            if stk[sp - 1] == 0 { t = 1; }
            stk[sp - 1] = t;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 27 {                      // STRNEQ: STREQ's byte-compare,
            y := stk[sp - 1];              // pushing the flip
            x := stk[sp - 2];
            mut eq := 0;
            if st.stl[x] == st.stl[y] {
                eq = 1;
                mut q := 0;
                while q < st.stl[x] {
                    if st.sb[st.sts[x] + q] as i64 != st.sb[st.sts[y] + q] as i64 {
                        eq = 0;
                    }
                    q += 1;
                }
            }
            stk[sp - 2] = 1 - eq;
            sp -= 1;
            pc += 1;
        }
        if op == 28 {                      // ITOS: pop an integer, append
            mut x := stk[sp - 1];          // its decimal image as a fresh
            base := st.cur[1];             // entry, push the entry's index
            mut len := 0;
            if x < 0 {
                st.sb[base] = 45 as u8;
                len = 1;
                x = -x;
            }
            mut m := 1;
            while x / m >= 10 { m = m * 10; }
            while m > 0 {
                st.sb[base + len] = (48 + (x / m) % 10) as u8;
                len += 1;
                m = m / 10;
            }
            ni := st.cur[0];
            st.sts[ni] = base;
            st.stl[ni] = len;
            st.cur[0] = ni + 1;
            st.cur[1] = base + len;
            stk[sp - 1] = ni;
            pc += 1;
        }
    }
    stk[0]
}

// The whole pipeline in one call: tokens in, code out, value from the VM.
fn show(src: str, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, code: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64,
        fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, nb: *u8, ss: *i64, sl: *i64,
        st: Strs) {
    lex(src.ptr as *u8, src.len as i64, k, v, ln, nb, ss, sl, st);
    r := parse_program(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, a, env, st, 0, code, 0);
    words := emit1(code, r.cp, 0);         // HALT closes the program
    put("{src}\n");                        // echo first: a program's prints
    val := run(code, stk, vars, st);       // land under its source
    put("  ->  {val}   [{words} code words]\n");
}

// The selfhost optic, at toy scale: compile and run a program this
// binary does NOT embed. The audited wrapper owns every kernel
// crossing (open/sbrk/read/close); the same single lexer that eats
// the embedded demos eats the file's bytes, because it never wanted a
// str in the first place — just bytes and a length. Missing file =
// a graceful skip (the fsio pattern; in-OS the file comes from /mnt).
#[caps(syscall)]
fn run_file(path: str, k: *i64, v: *i64, ln: *i64, code: *i64, stk: *i64, vars: *i64,
            fe: *i64, fp: *i64, fpc: *i64, a: Ast, env: Scope, nb: *u8, ss: *i64, sl: *i64,
            st: Strs) {
    fd := sys_open(path.ptr as *u8, 0, 0);
    if fd < 0 {
        put("(no toy demo file - file demo skipped)\n");
        return;
    }
    buf := sys_sbrk(1024) as *u8;
    len := sys_read(fd, buf, 1024);
    sys_close(fd);
    if len < 1 {
        put("(no toy demo file - file demo skipped)\n");
        return;
    }
    put("compiling n_toy_demo.n from disk:\n");
    lex(buf, len, k, v, ln, nb, ss, sl, st);
    r := parse_program(k, v, ln, fe, fp, fpc, a, env, st, 0, code, 0);
    words := emit1(code, r.cp, 0);         // HALT closes the program
    val := run(code, stk, vars, st);
    put("  ->  {val}   [{words} code words]\n");
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    k := alloc_words(96);
    v := alloc_words(96);
    code := alloc_words(256);
    stk := alloc_words(64);
    vars := alloc_words(64);               // slots are symbol indexes
    fe := alloc_words(64);
    fp := alloc_words(256);                // 64 fns x up to 4 param slots
    fpc := alloc_words(64);
    nb := alloc_bytes(256);                // the interned-names buffer
    ss := alloc_words(64);
    sl := alloc_words(64);
    ln := alloc_words(96);                 // per-token source lines (rung 2)
    ast := Ast{ nk: alloc_words(96), nl: alloc_words(96), nr: alloc_words(96),
                nv: alloc_words(96), na: alloc_words(384),
                nline: alloc_words(96),
                nt: alloc_words(96) };     // the TYPE COLUMN (0 int, 1 str)
    env := Scope{ bv: alloc_words(64), vt: alloc_words(64) };
    st := Strs{ sb: alloc_bytes(256),      // the string table: bytes + spans
                sts: alloc_words(32), stl: alloc_words(32),
                cur: alloc_words(2) };     // + the shared write cursors
    put("a type column over the toy - lex -> parse -> check -> fold -> emit -> run:\n");
    // Braces are escaped so they read as text, not interpolation (§2.5).
    show("fn max(a, b) \{ m := 0; if a > b \{ m := a; \} else \{ m := b; \} m \} max(3, 9) + max(7, 2)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    show("fn gcd(a, b) \{ r := a; if b > 0 \{ r := gcd(b, a % b); \} r \} gcd(48, 18)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    show("fn fact(n) \{ r := 1; if n > 1 \{ r := n * fact(n - 1); \} r \} fact(5)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // Folding, visible: unfolded this program emits 19 code words; the
    // fold pass collapses 2*3+4 to 10 and 10-8 to 2 at compile time and
    // 10 words remain. (The flagship demos above fold nothing — their
    // constants sit alone as call arguments — so their counts held.)
    show("d := 2 * 3 + 4; // constants fold\nd * (10 - 8)",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // Dead-arm elimination, visible: the condition folds to 0, the if
    // dissolves, and 7 words remain of an unfolded 18. The dead arm was
    // still checked first — dead code must be legal code.
    show("if 1 > 2 \{ x := 999; \} y := 5; y",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // The toy speaks, and strings are VALUES: one is bound to a name,
    // loaded back, and printed — same stack, same vars table, same
    // LOAD as any integer, with the type column picking PRINTS. The
    // computed (and folded — PUSH 42) number takes PRINT instead:
    // one syntax, two opcodes, decided by the checker.
    show("greet := \"hello from the toy\"; print greet; print len(greet); print 6 * 7; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // The type column's second dividend: == over two strings resolves
    // to STREQ (and != to STRNEQ — the equality pair is symmetric) —
    // and since both yield an int, string equality composes with
    // plain conditions for free.
    show("name := \"nyx\"; if name == \"nyx\" \{ print \"the goddess\"; \} print name == \"day\"; print name != \"day\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // And + MAKES strings: the first print concatenates at RUN time (a
    // load meets a literal — the VM appends a fresh table entry), the
    // second at COMPILE time (two literals — the fold pass appends
    // through the same cursor and PRINTS a ready-made entry), and the
    // result chains into == like any string.
    show("who := \"nyx\"; print who + \" rules\"; print \"nyx\" + \"os\"; print who + \"os\" == \"nyxos\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // INTERPOLATION — the real lexer's hardest trick, in the toy: a
    // string splits at its braces (HEAD/MID/TAIL segment tokens, the
    // lexer switching to expression mode inside each hole) and the
    // parser desugars the pieces into the plain + chain the type
    // column already compiles — an int hole becomes text through ITOS,
    // a str hole inserts as itself. The second print is all-constant:
    // ITOS folds the digits, the CONCATs collapse, and the entire
    // string becomes ONE literal at compile time.
    show("n := 6 * 7; w := \"toy\";\nprint \"the \{w\} says \{n\}\";\nprint \"\{1 + 2\} and \{40 + 2\}\"; 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // The rest of the comparison set, each its own opcode.
    show("n := 7; print n != 7; print n <= 7; print n >= 9; print !(n >= 9); 0",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // else-if chains: free structure — the else slot held a statement
    // all along, and a nested if is a statement.
    show("g := 72;\nif g >= 90 \{ print \"gold\"; \} else if g >= 60 \{ print \"silver\"; \} else \{ print \"bronze\"; \}\ng",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // And a program this binary does not carry: read off the disk,
    // compiled by the same pipeline, run by the same VM.
    run_file("/tmp/n_toy_demo.n",
             k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    // The broken program runs LAST (fail() ends the process — one failing
    // demo per run). It PARSES cleanly; what is wrong is a TYPE: a string
    // fed to +. The check pass refuses it with the operator's line — a
    // bug the machine could never catch (an index adds like any number).
    // Earlier failing demos rotate through this slot as new checks land;
    // the unknown-variable and expect-point refusals still stand guard,
    // all ending through the same fail().
    put("and a deliberately broken program:\n");
    show("x := 1;\nprint x + \"oops\";\nx",
         k, v, ln, code, stk, vars, fe, fp, fpc, ast, env, nb, ss, sl, st);
    0
}
